.TH NIWM 1 "2026" "niwm 0.6.0" "NiWM manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
niwm-x11, niwmctl \- lightweight tiling and floating window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B niwm-x11
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B niwmctl
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
NiWM arranges windows on nine tags. A window can sit on several tags,
and several tags can be viewed at once. Each tag keeps its own layout:
.BR tile ,
.BR monocle ,
.B grid
or
.BR float .
Any window can float on its own.
.PP
NiWM has one command language. The config file is a list of commands,
key bindings run commands, and
.B niwmctl
sends a command to the running window manager.
.SH FILES
The first file found is used:
.IP \(bu 2
.B $NIWM_CONFIG
.IP \(bu 2
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/niwm/niwmrc
.IP \(bu 2
.B ~/.config/niwm/niwmrc
.IP \(bu 2
.B /etc/niwm/niwmrc
.PP
With none of them present, a built-in copy of the stock niwmrc is used.
.SH SETTING UP NIWMRC
NiWM does not create
.B ~/.config/niwm
for you. Until you make it, the system-wide
.B /etc/niwm/niwmrc
is used.
.PP
Your own niwmrc
.B replaces
the stock one; it is not added on top. A file holding only
.B set gap 10
leaves you with no key bindings at all. So start from a copy of the
stock file and edit it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
mkdir \-p ~/.config/niwm
cp /etc/niwm/niwmrc ~/.config/niwm/niwmrc
nano ~/.config/niwm/niwmrc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Save, then press
.B mod+ctrl+r
(or run
.BR "niwmctl reload" )
to apply it without logging out.
.PP
Every line is one command, and lines starting with
.B #
are comments. Any line can be tried live first, then copied into the
file once it looks right:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
niwmctl set gap 10
niwmctl set focus_color #3355ff
.fi
.RE
.PP
An example of lines you might change or add:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# look
set gap 10
set border 3
set focus_color #c23b3b
# leave room for a bar
set pad_top 18

# use alt instead of the super key
set mod alt

# programs (set terminal goes above the autostart lines)
set terminal lxterminal
bind mod+b spawn midori
bind mod+e spawn pcmanfm
bind Print spawn scrot

# run once at login (ignored on reload)
autostart feh \-\-bg\-fill ~/wallpaper.png

# windows
rule class=mpv float
rule class=Midori tag 2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use
.B niwmctl clients
to find a window's class for a
.B rule
line.
.PP
Mistakes in niwmrc never stop NiWM: the bad line is skipped and the rest
of the file still loads. The error is printed to stderr as
.IR "niwm: file:line: message" .
To keep those messages, start NiWM from
.B ~/.xinitrc
like this:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
exec niwm\-x11 2> "$HOME/.niwm.log"
.fi
.RE
.SH DEFAULT KEYS
.B mod
is Super unless
.B set mod
says otherwise.
.TP
.B mod+Return
terminal (see TERMINAL below)
.TP
.B mod+s
show or hide the scratchpad terminal
.TP
.B mod+r
dmenu_run
.TP
.B mod+p
nibar's applications menu
.TP
.B mod+j / mod+k
focus next / previous
.TP
.B mod+shift+j / mod+shift+k
swap with next / previous
.TP
.B mod+ctrl+Return
make focused window the master
.TP
.B mod+space
next layout (t, m, g: tile, monocle, grid; shift+f: float)
.TP
.B mod+ctrl+space
toggle floating
.TP
.B mod+f
toggle fullscreen
.TP
.B mod+h / mod+l
shrink / grow master area
.TP
.B mod+1..9
view tag;
.B shift
moves the window,
.B ctrl
adds the tag to the view,
.B ctrl+shift
toggles the window on that tag
.TP
.B mod+Tab
previous view
.TP
.B mod+period / mod+comma
select the next / previous monitor
.TP
.B mod+shift+period / mod+shift+comma
send the focused window to the next / previous monitor
.TP
.B mod+button1 / mod+button3
move / resize (floats the window)
.TP
.B mod+shift+c
close window
.TP
.B mod+ctrl+r
reload niwmrc
.TP
.B mod+shift+q
quit
.SH COMMANDS
Run
.B niwmctl help
for the full list. The main ones:
.TP
.BI spawn " cmd"
run a shell command
.TP
.BI autostart " cmd"
run once at login (ignored on reload)
.TP
.BI scratchpad " [cmd]"
show or hide the scratchpad, a floating window that drops down at the
top of the screen. If there is none yet,
.I cmd
is started and the next window that opens becomes the scratchpad. It
then hides and comes back, still running, and follows you to whatever
tag you are on. Pressing it while the scratchpad is shown but not
focused focuses it.
.TP
\fBview\fR \fIN\fR|all|prev, \fBtag\fR \fIN\fR|all, \fBtoggleview\fR \fIN\fR, \fBtoggletag\fR \fIN\fR
.TP
.BR "layout " tile|monocle|grid|float|next|prev
.TP
\fBmfact\fR 0.6|+0.05, \fBnmaster\fR 2|+1
.TP
.BI set " key value"
border, gap, focus_color, normal_color, urgent_color, smart_borders,
pad_top, pad_bottom, mod, tags, layout, mfact, nmaster,
focus_follows_mouse,
.B swallow
(yes or no, see SWALLOWING),
.B terminal
(sets $TERMINAL) and
.B scratchpad_size
(width and height in percent, written like the default 60x45)
.TP
.BI bind " combo command"
e.g.
.B bind mod+shift+Return spawn st
.TP
.BI rule " match... action"
match with class=, instance= (exact) or title= (substring); action is
.BR float ,
.BR tile ,
.BI tag " N" ,
.BI monitor " N" ,
.B scratchpad
(the window becomes the scratchpad),
.B noswallow
(it never swallows its terminal) or
.B terminal
(it counts as a terminal for swallowing)
.TP
.BR kill " [\fIid\fR]"
close the focused window, or the window with that id
.TP
.BI activate " id"
view the window's tag if it is hidden, then focus it
.TP
.B query
current tags, layout and focused window, one fact per line, for bars
and scripts
.TP
.B clients
every window: id, tags, class and title
.TP
.B subscribe
keep the connection open and print a full state block every time
something changes (see SCRIPTING)
.TP
.BI emit " text"
send
.BI "event " text
to every subscriber. The stock
.B mod+p
uses
.B emit menu
to open nibar's menu.
.SH MONITORS
NiWM finds monitors through RandR, and each one has its own nine tags,
layouts and bar space, like awesome. Monitor 1 is the primary, then the
others from left to right. New windows open on the selected monitor,
which is the one holding the focused window, or the one under the
pointer when you move it over an empty desktop.
.PP
.B focusmon
selects a monitor and
.B tagmon
sends the focused window to one (both take
.BR next ,
.B prev
or a number); a rule can send a program's windows to a monitor with
.BI monitor " N" \fR.
Dragging a floating window onto another monitor moves it there.
Unplugging a monitor moves its windows to monitor 1.
.PP
.BR "xrandr \-\-setmonitor"
can split one screen into virtual monitors, which is handy for a wide
screen, or for trying this out on a laptop:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
xrandr \-\-setmonitor L 683/181x768/203+0+0 eDP\-1
xrandr \-\-setmonitor R 683/181x768/203+683+0 none
.fi
.RE
.PP
Here a 1366x768 screen on output eDP\-1 becomes two halves (the numbers
after each / are sizes in millimetres; any will do). Find your output's
name with
.BR "xrandr \-q" .
Undo it with
.B xrandr \-\-delmonitor L
and
.BR "xrandr \-\-delmonitor R" .
NiWM follows these changes as they happen.
.PP
Each
.BR nibar (1)
sits on one monitor; start one per monitor, e.g.
.B autostart nibar \-m 2
for the second.
.SH SWALLOWING
A window started from a terminal, such as
.B mpv video.mp4
or an image viewer, swallows the terminal: it takes the terminal's
exact place in the layout and the terminal hides. When the window
closes, the terminal comes back in the same place with the focus, and
nibar lists the window in the terminal's place meanwhile.
.PP
NiWM decides this from processes: the terminal's process has to be an
ancestor of the new window's process (looked up in
.BR /proc ).
Terminals such as lxterminal open every window from one process; then
the terminal window you were using, the focused or most recently focused
one, is swallowed.
.PP
Only plain tiled windows swallow. Dialogs and floating windows, windows
a rule sends to another tag, terminals started from terminals, and the
scratchpad are left alone. While a terminal is swallowed, other programs
started from it open normally.
.PP
Common terminals are recognised by their class, as is whatever
$TERMINAL names. Use
.B rule class=X terminal
for another one,
.B rule class=X noswallow
for a program that should open beside its terminal instead, and
.B set swallow no
to turn swallowing off.
.PP
The window's process comes from
.BR _NET_WM_PID .
For programs that don't set it, NiWM asks the X server through the XRes
extension, when built with xcb-res.
.SH TERMINAL
At startup NiWM sets
.B $TERMINAL
to the first of \%x\-terminal\-emulator, \%lxterminal, xfce4\-terminal,
alacritty, foot, st, urxvt and xterm that is installed, unless
$TERMINAL already names an installed program.
.B set terminal
in niwmrc picks one yourself. mod+Return, the scratchpad and nibar's
menu all use it.
.SH BARS
NiWM honours
.B _NET_WM_STRUT
and
.BR _NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL ,
so a dock (nibar, lemonbar, polybar, tint2) gets its own space and
tiled windows never go under it. The stock niwmrc starts
.BR nibar (1).
.PP
A dock chooses what happens when a window goes fullscreen by setting
.B _NIWM_LAYER
to
.B below
(it is covered, the default),
.B above
(it stays on top) or
.B beside
(fullscreen windows leave its space free). A dock with
.B _NET_WM_STATE_ABOVE
counts as above. nibar sets this from
.B layer
in nibarc.
.PP
.B reload
also sends
.B event reload
to subscribers, so nibar re-reads nibarc at the same time.
.SH SCRIPTING
.B niwmctl subscribe
prints a block like this, then a new one after every change:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
begin
tags 9
view 1
occupied 5
urgent 0
layout tile
colors c23b3b 3a3a3a e0a030
monitor 0 0 0 1366 768 1 5 0 tile *
client 60000c 4 \- 0 XTerm<tab>title
client 40000c 1 f 0 Midori<tab>title
end
.fi
.RE
.PP
.BR view ,
.B occupied
and
.B urgent
are tag bit masks in hex (5 = tags 1 and 3), for the selected monitor.
Each
.B monitor
line gives its number (0 = the primary), x, y, width, height, view,
occupied, urgent, layout, and * if it is selected. After
.B client
come the window id, its tag mask and its flags:
.B f
focused,
.B F
floating,
.B S
fullscreen,
.B u
urgent,
.B s
scratchpad,
.B w
swallowing a terminal, or
.B \-
for none. Then its monitor number, the class, a tab, and the title.
.PP
For example, a script can print the focused title every time it
changes:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
niwmctl subscribe | while read \-r kind id tags flags rest; do
    case $kind$flags in clientf*) echo "$rest" ;; esac
done
.fi
.RE
.SH SIGNALS
SIGHUP reloads the config. SIGTERM and SIGINT exit cleanly.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B NIWM_SOCKET
is set for every program NiWM starts and tells niwmctl where to connect.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nibar (1)
